{"message": "Honestly, I lean toward empirical evidence, though I don't think it's a clean either/or. When I'm forming a view about something that can be tested or measured, I want the data to do most of the work, and I get uneasy when someone's strong intuition is treated as enough. Intuitions are useful as a source of hypotheses, but they have a track record of being confidently wrong in ways we don't notice from the inside.

That said, I don't trust pure data without reasoning either. Numbers don't interpret themselves, and many of the questions I care most about (what counts as fairness, what a good life is, what we owe each other) can't be settled by evidence alone. There, I rely on careful philosophical argument, and I try to be explicit about which premises I'm taking on faith.

So if I had to name my actual inclination: evidence first, reasoning to connect it to what matters, and intuition treated as a clue to check rather than a verdict. I'm curious where you land on this, since you asked the question."}
